1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,148
We're starting from a clean Windows sandbox — nothing installed yet.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,280
First, the TechyGeeksHome website.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:20,352
Here's the DiskGeek page — a free disk space analyser, one click from downloading.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,768
Straight to the download.

5
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:36,752
Because it isn't code-signed yet, Windows shows a SmartScreen warning — expected for a small, independent tool. Clicking Keep lets it through.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,548
Running the installer — DiskGeek installs to Program Files, shared by everyone on the PC.

7
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,408
A quick desktop shortcut option, then it's ready to install.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,868
One click confirms everything, and the install itself takes just a few seconds.

9
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:08,792
And here it is — DiskGeek, up and running for the first time.

10
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:17,160
Point it at a folder — here, the whole Program Files drive — and click Scan.

11
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:27,832
In under a second — six hundred and eighty seven files mapped, sorted by size right away.

12
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:35,400
Switch to Treemap, and the biggest space-users jump right out.

13
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:50,176
Exact Duplicates finds repeated files byte for byte — four groups here, wasting over a megabyte, with the oldest copy kept unchecked by default so nothing's deleted by accident.

14
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:57,232
Similar Images catches near-duplicate photos, even when the files don't match exactly.

15
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:11,024
Search narrows things down instantly — filtering by name, size, or date, live as you type. Select a batch of results and rename them all in one pass.

16
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,620
Save a snapshot of any scan, and compare it against a later one to see exactly what's changed since.

17
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:34,800
Permissions shows the owner and full access list for any file or folder — handy for tracking down what's locking a system folder.

18
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:40,312
Export to CSV or HTML, any time.

19
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,172
A built-in update check confirms you're always on the latest version.

20
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,580
And the About screen makes it official — DiskGeek, version 1.0, brought to you by TechyGeeksHome.

21
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:08,300
That HTML export opens right in your browser — a clean, shareable breakdown of exactly where the space went.

22
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:17,888
That's DiskGeek — free to download, install, and use.
